\label{q:maximal.angle}
Given a hyperbolic cone-surface \(X\in\mathcal{T} (S,\underline{p})\), how large can the cone
angles at the marked points be under triangulability constraints?
More precisely:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item[(a)] for a fixed triangulation \(G\in\text{Tri}(S,\underline{p})\), how large can the cone angles at the marked points be among cone-metrics \(X\in\mathcal{T}(G)\)?
  \item[(b)] how large can the cone angles at the marked points be along the
  universally triangulable locus \(\mathcal{T}_{\rm u.t.}(S,\underline{p})\subset\mathcal{T} (S,\underline{p})\)?
\end{enumerate}

\label{thm:adm.cone.angle}
The following statements hold:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item If \(n = g = 1\), then \(\Theta(S,\underline{p}) = 2\pi\) and \(\mathcal{T} (S,\underline{p}) = \mathcal{T}_{\rm u.t.}(S,\underline{p})\).  Otherwise, \(\Theta(S,\underline{p}) = \pi\).
  \item The value \(\Theta(S,\underline{p})\) cannot be achieved by any element of \(\mathcal{T}_{\rm u.t.}(S,\underline{p})\), except in the case \(n = 1\) and \(g \ge 2\).
\end{enumerate}

\label{cor:once.punctured.torus.global}
If \(g = n = 1\), then for every triangulation \(G \in \text{Tri}(S,\underline{p})\) the shear--radius map \(\mathcal{SR}_G : \mathcal{T} (S,\underline{p}) \longrightarrow \Lambda_G \) is a homeomorphism.  
In other words, the shear--radius coordinates are global coordinates for \(\mathcal{T}(S,\underline{p})\).

An {\em (oriented) arc} on \((S,\underline{p})\) is a continuous map \(\alpha \colon [0,1] \to S\) with endpoints \(\alpha(0), \alpha(1) \in \underline{p}\), such that the image \(\alpha((0,1))\) is contained in \(\dot{S} = S \setminus \underline{p}\). 
We allow \(\alpha(0) = \alpha(1)\).
The arc \(\alpha\) is {\em simple} if it is injective on \([0,1)\).
Two arcs \(\alpha_0\) and \(\alpha_1\) on \((S,\underline{p})\) are {\em homotopic relative to \(\underline{p}\)} if there exists a continuous family \(\{ f_t \colon [0,1] \to S \}_{t \in [0,1]}\) such that
\begin{itemize}
  \item \(f_0 = \alpha_0\) and \(f_1 = \alpha_1\);
  \item \(f_t(0)\) and \(f_t(1)\) are constant in \(\underline{p}\) for all \(t \in [0,1]\);
  \item \(f_t((0,1)) \subset \dot{S}\) for all \(t \in [0,1]\).
\end{itemize}
We say that $\gamma$ is {\em essential} if it is not homotopic (relative to $\underline{p}$) in $S$ to a point.
We denote by \(\mathcal{A}(S,\underline{p})\) the set of homotopy classes (relative to \(\underline{p}\)) of essential arcs on \((S,\underline{p})\).

\label{def:weak.top.func.space}
Let \(\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{\mathcal{A} \cup \mathcal{C}}\) denote the space of functions \(f \colon \mathcal{A} \cup \mathcal{C} \to \mathbb{R}\).
We equip \(\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{\mathcal{A} \cup \mathcal{C}}\) with the {\em weak topology} (or topology of pointwise convergence) defined as follows.
For any \(f_0 \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{\mathcal{A} \cup \mathcal{C}}\), any finite subset \(\underline{\beta} \subset \mathcal{A} \cup \mathcal{C}\) and any collection of positive numbers \(\underline{\epsilon} = (\epsilon_{\beta})_{\beta \in \underline{\beta}}\), we set
\[
  \mathcal{U}_{f_0}(\underline{\beta}, \underline{\epsilon})
  :=
  \left\{
    f \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{\mathcal{A} \cup \mathcal{C}}
    \;\middle|\;
    \lvert f(\beta) - f_0(\beta) \rvert < \epsilon_{\beta}
    \text{ for all } \beta \in \underline{\beta}
  \right\}.
\]
The sets \(\mathcal{U}_{f_0}(\underline{\beta}, \underline{\epsilon})\) form a neighbourhood basis of \(f_0\) and hence define a topology on \(\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{\mathcal{A} \cup \mathcal{C}}\), called the weak topology.
Similarly, for the set \(\mathcal{S}\subset \mathcal{A} \cup \mathcal{C}\) of simple closed curve classes,  we denote by \(\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{\mathcal{S}}\) the space of functions \(f:\mathcal{S}\to\mathbb{R}\),
equipped with the weak topology defined by the analogous neighbourhood basis
\(\mathcal{U}_{f_0}(\underline{\beta},\underline{\epsilon})\), where \(f_0\in\mathbb{R}^{\mathcal{S}}_{\geq 0}\) and \(\underline{\beta}\subset\mathcal{S}\) is finite.

\label{rem:MFp.convention}
For \([F]\in\mathcal{MF} (S,\underline{p})\) we adopt the following standing assumptions, which match the domain of our coordinate systems:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item the intersection number \(i([F],[\beta])\) is finite for all \([\beta]\in\mathcal{A} \cup \mathcal{C}\);
  \item for each marked point \(p_i\), the restriction of \(F\) to a neighbourhood of \(p_i\) carries positive transverse measure.
\end{enumerate}

    \label{def:MF.triv.pt}  
Let $\Delta$ be a topological triangle (Definition~\ref{def:top.triang.}), and let $F$ be a measured foliation on $\Delta$.  
\begin{itemize}
    \item For $x\in\partial\Delta$, we say that $F$ is {\em trivial around $x$} if there is a neighbourhood $U$ of $x$ in $\Delta$ such that all leaves of $F$ in $U$ are arcs with endpoints on the two sides of $x$ along $\partial\Delta$.
    \item For $x\in\mathrm{int}(\Delta)$, we say that $F$ is {\em trivial around $x$} if there is a neighbourhood $U$ of $x$ in $\Delta$ on which the leaves of $F$ are simple closed curves encircling $x$ (the standard local model for measured foliations).
\end{itemize}

\label{rem:MF.Gperp.to.MFp}
By construction, a measured foliation totally transverse to a triangulation \(G\in\text{Tri}(S,\underline{p})\) is transverse to every edge of \(G\), trivial around each vertex, and hence trivial around the set \(\underline{p}\) of marked points.
In particular, we have \(\mathcal{MF}(G^{\perp}) \subset \mathcal{MF} (S,\underline{p})\).

\label{dual.tt.G}
Let \(G\in\text{Tri}(S,\underline{p})\).
A train track \(\tau\) on \((S,\underline{p})\) is said to be {\em dual} to \(G\) if, for each face \(f\in F(G)\), the intersection \(\tau\cap f\) has exactly three branches in the interior of \(f\) bounding a trigon (a disk with three spikes) and exactly one branch intersecting each edge of \(f\), as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:one.junction}.
By \cite[Sec.~2]{PP93} and \cite[Sec.~5]{PP05}, there exists a unique train track dual to \(G\) up to equivalence.
We denote this equivalence class, and any fixed representative in it, by \(\tau_G\).

